1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,750
Twelve hundred books a month.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:04,050
Thousands of authors.

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:06,590
And every question answered by hand.

4
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:13,930
That means slow replies, answers that don't match, and a backlog that grows every busy week.

5
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:16,950
Meet BookLeaf Support.

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:18,990
Built to fix exactly that.

7
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,490
First, the manual work.

8
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:30,130
The AI now reads every new ticket, sorts it into the right category, sets its priority, and drafts a reply.

9
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,150
Urgent money problems no longer get buried.

10
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,530
The most urgent, oldest tickets always come first.

11
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:45,770
Every draft follows BookLeaf's own policies and tone, so every author gets the same right answer.

12
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,850
And it uses the author's real royalty data, fetched live from BookLeaf.

13
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:53,870
Never guessed, never made up.

14
00:00:55,800 --> 00:01:01,330
Authors see every ticket, its status, and every reply, live, without refreshing.

15
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:09,310
A fact checker blocks any amount or date that isn't in the records, and a person always sends the reply.

16
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,470
If the AI goes down, tickets still arrive, get sorted, and get answered.

17
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:26,330
Then I went beyond the brief: help inside the author's own dashboard, chat and voice in one conversation, and tickets that fill themselves as you talk.

18
00:01:27,300 --> 00:01:32,770
It plugs into any product with one script tag and one endpoint, for any company.

19
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:40,990
It runs on fast, low-cost models, sends only what each answer needs, and shows admins exactly what the AI costs.

20
